    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Farm, garden and bin",
     "texto": [
      "Today we talk about animals, plants and recycling.\n\n- **The farm:** the **gallina** lays eggs and the **gallo** wakes everyone up. The sheep and its young, the **cordero**. Also the **conejo**, the cow, the pig and the horse.\n- **The jungle:** the lion, the **tigre**, the elephant, the snake and the **cocodrilo** are wild animals. A dog or a cat is a domestic animal.\n- **The garden and the countryside:** the **césped** is mowed and watered. A tree has roots, a **tronco**, branches and leaves. Outside the village there is a **prado** for the animals and there are **cultivos**.\n- **The weather:** **granizo** can ruin the crops. A heatwave lasts several days.\n- **Recycling:** each **contenedor** has its colour. **Vidrio** goes in the green one. **Pilas** are toxic and are taken to a special point."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Male and female",
     "texto": [
      "Many animals have one word for the male and another for the female:\n\n- **el gallo / la gallina** · **el toro / la vaca** · **el caballo / la yegua**\n- **el perro / la perra** · **el gato / la gata** · **el conejo / la coneja** · **el tigre / la tigresa**\n\nOthers have one single form for both: **el cocodrilo**, **la jirafa**, **la serpiente**. To be precise you add **macho** or **hembra**: un cocodrilo hembra.\n\nThe article shows the gender of the word, not the sex of the animal: **la jirafa** can be male."
     ],
